On 11/30/2013 04:33 PM, Thomas Kluyver wrote:
> On 30 November 2013 13:11, Laurent Gautier <lgautier at gmail.com 
> <mailto:lgautier at gmail.com>> wrote:
>
>     Yet, I am not convinced that a BSD-type license is appropriate
>     here. The
>     liberal licensing types can easily use the project in isolation,
>     either
>     with a module calling rpy2
>
>
> If your intention is that people can write code to call rpy2 
> (installed separately) without being bound by the terms of the GPL, 
> you may want to make this clear in the public description of the 
> licensing, because that's not clearly the normal interpretation of the 
> GPL. I think this is permissible - code written in R is not bound by 
> the GPL simply because it uses the language, so it would be hard to 
> argue that code written with Python syntax to perform the same 
> operations using R should be bound by the GPL.

Is there really a need to write all permissible usages ? The list can 
get really long.

My intention is to release rpy2 with a license that appears to comply 
with R's license, as the majority here seems to believe that it should 
be the case. I am only pointing out here that proponents of BSD-type 
licensing can try out their interpretation of licensing with their own 
library calling rpy2.

Also, keeping the full sentence, or even better the full email, when 
quoting someone could be perceived less misleading.

L.


